  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to employ a convex relaxation of the non-convex minimax problem by transforming it into a convex optimization problem that can be efficiently solved with gradient-based methods. By approximating the maximum overlap integral with a smooth, convex function, we can reformulate the problem as a constrained convex optimization, allowing us to leverage efficient optimization algorithms. This approach avoids the pitfalls of direct gradient methods on non-convex objectives and ensures numerical stability. The main limitation is the need to carefully select the smoothing parameter and the trade-off between approximation accuracy and computational efficiency, which are managed through adaptive parameter selection.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nLet $ h \\in [0,1]^n $ be the piecewise constant function to be optimized, where $ n = n\\_points $. The objective is to minimize:\n$$\nC_5 = \\max_k \\int h(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx\n$$\nsubject to:\n- $ \\sum h_i \\cdot dx = 1 $, where $ dx = 2/n $\n- $ 0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1 $ for all $ i $\n\nWe reformulate the problem by introducing a scalar variable $ C $ and reformulating the maximization as a constraint:\n$$\n\\forall k, \\quad \\int h(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx \\leq C\n$$\nThis is approximated using a convex surrogate function:\n$$\n\\mathrm{relax}(C) = \\log\\left(\\sum_{k} \\exp(c_k/C)\\right)\n$$\nwhere $ c_k $ is the discretized cross-correlation at lag $ k $, scaled by $ dx $. The convex objective becomes:\n$$\n\\min_{h, C} \\quad \\mathrm{relax}(C)\n$$\nwith constraints ensuring $ h $ stays within bounds and the integral constraint is satisfied.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Parent Validation:\n- Check if initial_h_values is valid (within bounds and satisfies sum constraint). Scale if necessary.\n- Ensure dx = 2 / n_points and that the sum constraint is met with a tolerance of $ 10^{-8} $.\n\nb. Initialization:\n- Start with initial_h_values or generate a uniform [0,1] array, then scale to meet the sum constraint.\n\nc. Objective Function:\n- Compute the full cross-correlation $ cc = \\text{np.correlate}(h, 1-h, mode='full') $.\n- Calculate $ c_k = cc[k] * dx $ for each lag $ k $.\n- Compute the relaxation $ \\mathrm{relax}(C) = \\log(\\sum \\exp(c_k/C)) $.\n\nd. Gradient Calculation:\n- For each $ h_i $, compute derivatives of $ \\mathrm{relax}(C) $ with respect to $ h_i $ using the chain rule.\n- Each $ h_i $ contributes to multiple cross-correlation terms (for all $ k $ where $ i + k $ is within bounds).\n\ne. Optimization Loop:\n- Use scipy.optimize.minimize with L-BFGS-B solver.\n- Bounds: [0,1] for each $ h_i $.\n- Constraints: $ \\sum h_i = n/2 $ (equality constraint).\n- Initial guess: sampled from $ \\text{initial\\_h\\_values} $ or random uniform [0,1].\n- Max iterations: 200, tolerance: $ 10^{-7} $.\n- Monitor elapsed time and terminate early if budget is exceeded.\n\nf. Post-Processing:\n- Clip $ h $ to [0,1] and re-scale to meet the sum constraint.\n- Recalculate $ C_5 $ using the original evaluator.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\na. Default Settings:\n- $ n\\_points = 600 $ (balances accuracy with computational load).\n- Random seed: 42 (for reproducibility).\n- Initial guess: Start from initial_h_values or random uniform [0,1].\n\nb. Optimization Variations:\n- Adaptive sampling: Sample 100 initial points from uniform [0,1] and select the best.\n- Multi-start: Run 5 independent optimizations with different random seeds.\n\nc. Parameter Tuning:\n- Adjust tolerance based on elapsed time.\n- If stuck in a local minimum, restart with a different initial guess.\n\nd. Convergence Criteria:\n- Terminate if no improvement in objective over 10 iterations.\n- Use the best candidate encountered during optimization.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\na. Time Estimation:\n- L-BFGS-B with 600 samples: ~500s for 200 iterations.\n- Gradient computation: O(n) per iteration.\n- Total time budget: 600s (sufficient for 5 iterations with 300s safety margin).\n\nb. Deadline Monitoring:\n- Check elapsed time every 50 iterations.\n- If time remaining < 30s, terminate and return best candidate.\n\nc. Numerical Stability:\n- Clip gradients to prevent overflow.\n- Use log-sum-exp to avoid numerical instability in maximizing.\n\nd. Fallback Strategy:\n- If optimization fails, return initial_h_values.\n- If gradient computation fails, use finite difference approximation.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\na. Feasibility Checks:\n- All $ h[i] $ must be in [0,1].\n- $ \\sum h_i = n/2 $ (within $ 10^{-6} $ tolerance).\n\nb. Objective Consistency:\n- Recompute $ C5 $ using the original evaluator.\n- Compare with relaxed objective to ensure approximation accuracy.\n\nc. Sanity Checks:\n- Verify that all cross-correlation values are finite.\n- Check for degenerate cases where cross-correlation is zero.\n\nd. Return Contract:\n- Return $ (h\\_values, c5\\_bound, n\\_points) $.\n- Ensure $ h\\_values $ is a NumPy array.\n- $ c5\\_bound $ is the evaluated maximum of cross-correlation.",
